// NEGATIVE TEST — a `bool` scrutinee must be covered (N6-02).
//
// `true` and `false` are the whole domain of a `bool`, which is what makes a
// match on one checkable at all: with literal patterns the compiler can now see
// that this arm set leaves `false` with nowhere to go. Every other scrutinee
// type still goes unchecked without a catch-all — no finite set of integer or
// string literals exhausts an `i64` or a `String`, and refusing those is N6-10.
//
// The value form makes the cost concrete: with no arm taken, the temporary this
// `match` lowers to is never written and `v` reads whatever the zero-initialiser
// left there.
fn main() {
    let flag = 1 < 2;
    let v = match flag {
        true => 1,
    };
    print_int(v);
}
